Damien Neil wrote on Fri, 10 Dec 2004 01:31:40 -0800:

> A good scoring system should:
> - Reward progress towards the goal of ascending.

This should be the most important factor.

> - Not allow arbitrarily high scores through repetition of the same
> actions.
> - Have a theoretical maximum score that is impossible to reach in
> practice.
> - Rarely result in ties.

I'm not sure that this is an important consideration, especially for 
non-ascensions.

> - Encourage interesting behavior.

Not if said behavior is not skill-testing.  Unless you want high score 
to be about completing as many actions as possible from the mind-
numbing Stupid Ascension Tricks list.

> Perhaps structure the score as a combination of a number of different
> elements:
> 
> - A fixed point award for each new level reached, with bonuses for
> certain milestones.  

Check.  Again, this should only apply to non-ascensions;  we don't want 
to score one ascension *less* than normal because the player didn't 
bother to visit the Mines, Sokoban, Ludious.  

> Since the dungeon size is variable, adjust the
> bonuses for the ends of branches so completing a branch always gives
> the same score.  (40,000 points for the Dungeons of Doom, for example,
> payable in installments of 100 points for each level with the remainder
> awarded on reaching the Castle.)

Mmm.  If one player dies at a DL-29 Castle should he get the same score 
as a player who dies at a DL-27 Castle?  He did beat two more levels of 
the main dungeon before dying.  Opinions may vary.

> - Bonuses for certain accomplishments on the way to ascention. 
> (Completing the quest, defeating Vlad, ascending, etc.)

Check.  We can also include accomplishments not necessary for 
ascension, like completing Sokoban and fetching the luckstone.

> These might
> offer a speed bonus; the faster you accomplish each step, the more
> points you get.

That's an interesting idea.  If Wizard One beats the Dark One at turn 
10k, and then dies at the Castle, should his score be higher than 
Wizard Two, who beat the Dark One at turn 20k and then died at the 
Castle?  Opinions may vary on this one, too.

> - Experience score--a function of your total experience points,
> exponentially declining past a certain point.  Going from level 1 to
> level 20 will get you a nice bonus to your score.  Going from 30 to 50
> probably won't be worth the bother.

I would simply give a bonus for each character level from 2-13, and a 
big bonus for level 14.  Small bonuses could be awarded for reaching 
lvls 20, 25, 30; those aren't needed, but they do make your character 
more powerful, so they count as progress towards ascension.

> - Carnage score--points awarded for each monster killed.

I think this is already well-covered by giving points for experience 
levels gained.

> Killing many types of monster gets you many points.  Killing many monsters of the
> same type gets you ... fewer points

Why? 

> - Wealth--points awarded for zorkmids of wealth owned at the end of the
> game, decreasing exponentially with higher numbers of zorkmids.

Again, why?
   
> - Exploration score--a pool of bonus points, awarded based on the
> percentage of the dungeon which has been mapped, number of items
> identified, etc.

Sure, one can argue that identifying items is progress towards 
ascension.

> - Exit multiplier--your final score is multiplied by a constant, based
> on how you ended the game.  Ascention gives the best multiplier, while
> quitting gives the worst.

The problem is that the point awards you've suggested are decent for 
comparing two non-ascensions, but horrible for comparing two 
ascensions.  And we'd still have the problem that sometimes winning the 
game gives a lower score than losing the game.




